        <section id="abstract">
            <h2>1. Abstract</h2>
            <p>Despite rapid advancements in assistive technology, individuals with disabilities continue to face systemic barriers in accessing, adapting, and sustaining high-quality mobility and communication devices. Much like the legendary city of <span class="keyword-highlight">atlantis</span>, promising innovations remain submerged beneath fragmented funding pipelines, complex eligibility bureaucracies, and inadequate post-implementation support. This proposal outlines a three-phase mixed-methods study designed to bridge the gap between technological availability and real-world quality of life (QoL) outcomes. By integrating adaptive AI-driven device customization with a novel peer-support framework, Project ATLANTIS aims to reduce device abandonment rates by 40% and significantly improve participant-reported QoL metrics across physical independence, social participation, and psychological well-being domains.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="objectives">
            <h2>2. Research Objectives</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Primary Objective:</strong> Evaluate the impact of the Integrated Device Support Ecosystem (IDSE) on standardized QoL measures (WHOQOL-BREF, PAC-TEAM) over a 24-month period.</li>
                <li><strong>Secondary Objective 1:</strong> Develop and validate an open-source adaptive firmware that learns user interaction patterns to auto-calibrate assistive devices (wheelchairs, eye-tracking systems, communication boards).</li>
                <li><strong>Secondary Objective 2:</strong> Train and deploy a network of Community Constable Navigators to provide continuous, culturally competent assistance with device maintenance, insurance navigation, and peer mentorship.</li>
                <li><strong>Secondary Objective 3:</strong> Identify socioeconomic and geographic barriers that disproportionately affect rural and marginalized disability communities.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="methodology">
            <h2>3. Methodology</h2>
            <h3>3.1 Study Design</h3>
            <p>A quasi-experimental longitudinal design will be employed across 4 metropolitan and 2 rural recruitment sites. Participants will be randomized to either the intervention group (n=180) receiving the IDSE package or the control group (n=180) receiving standard care referrals.</p>

            <h3>3.2 The Community Constable Navigator Model</h3>
            <p>Central to this proposal is the deployment of trained Community <span class="keyword-highlight">constable</span> Navigators. Drawing inspiration from traditional community policing models that emphasize proximity, trust, and proactive assistance, our Navigator role focuses on consistent, proactive engagement rather than reactive crisis management. Each Navigator will be assigned a caseload of 15 participants, conducting biweekly check-ins, facilitating device troubleshooting, and coordinating with healthcare providers. Navigators will complete a 120-hour certification program covering disability etiquette, adaptive technology fundamentals, insurance advocacy, and trauma-informed communication.</p>

            <h3>3.3 Data Collection & Analysis</h3>
            <p>Data will be collected at baseline, 6, 12, 18, and 24 months. Quantitative metrics include device usage telemetry, QoL survey scores, and healthcare utilization records. Qualitative data will be gathered through semi-structured interviews and focus groups. Mixed-methods analysis will utilize hierarchical linear modeling (HLM) for longitudinal quantitative data and thematic analysis for qualitative transcripts.</p>
